WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.710
SPEAKER 1: Ladies and gentlemen,
welcome back from lunch.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.480
It's my pleasure
to now introduce

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.270
to you our speaker this
afternoon, Mollie Hemingway.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:12.420
Let me just say a few
words about Miss Hemingway.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.670
She is a senior
journalism fellow

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:19.050
here at Hillsdale College, a
senior editor at The Federalist

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.660
and a Fox News contributor.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.980
She's been a Phillips Foundation
journalism fellow, a Lincoln

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.630
fellow at the
Claremont Institute

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:31.380
for the Study of statesmanship,
and political philosophy,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.540
and a Eugene C
Pulliam distinguished

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.870
visiting fellow in journalism
here at the college.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.260
She has also written
numerous publications

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.180
for, including the
Wall Street Journal,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.470
the Claremont Review of Books,
USA Today, Ricochet, The Los

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.440
Angeles Times, The
Washington Post, The Federal

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.380
Times, and Christianity Today.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:57.300
She is the author of Trump
Versus the Media and co-author

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.980
of Justice on Trial--

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.920
The Kavanaugh Confirmation
and the Future

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.180
of the Supreme Court.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:07.346
Would you please welcome
Mollie Hemingway.

00:01:07.346 --> 00:01:19.940
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.785
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: Thank you.

00:01:22.785 --> 00:01:32.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:36.570
OK, you all are
completely ridiculous,

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:37.880
but thank you very much.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.710
It is such an honor
to be here and I

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:43.190
can't believe what we just got
to experience this morning.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:47.570
I was so moved by the music,
the readings, the speeches,

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.920
and I'm so glad that I
got to be part of it.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.130
And I was kind of wondering
if the students realized

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:55.280
what they were participating in
or if it will take a few more

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.290
years before they
realized the significance

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.450
and how special today was.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:03.320
But we're here celebrating
the 175th anniversary

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:04.700
of Hillsdale College.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.590
But it is a year with many
important anniversaries.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.890
For instance, it is
the 25th anniversary

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.990
of Al Gore saying one of my
favorite things he ever said.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:21.410
And you realize that
students here on campus

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:24.050
are too young to have
actually experienced Al Gore,

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.820
they weren't alive in the 1990s.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.680
So they might not
remember that he

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.250
was known when he was
vice president for saying

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:35.000
really ridiculous
things, and saying

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.250
some pretty ridiculous
things in the years

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.430
after being vice
president, as well.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.812
He predicted the Earth's
ice caps would melt by 2014.

00:02:44.812 --> 00:02:46.770
So that's another really
important anniversary.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:52.310
We've had five years with no
ice caps, as Al Gore predicted.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.440
But most of his
farcical observations

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.460
are best forgotten, but one has
proved prescient and revealing,

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.560
if unintentionally so.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.040
In the process of
praising America's legacy

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.800
of ethnic tolerance
or aspiring thinking

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.180
that we could do
better in the future,

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.200
he said, "America
can be e pluribus

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:17.360
unum," which he translated
as, out of one, many.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.580
It was what in DC was
called a classic Kinsley

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.570
gaffe, coined by deceased
commentator Michael Kinsley.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:32.100
That's when a politician reveals
some truth that they did not

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.920
intend to admit.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.870
And over a quarter
century ago, he

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:40.470
was inadvertently saying
something that has subsequently

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.510
become something of
an article of faith

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.670
among many on the left.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:48.370
Having a shared
identity as Americans,

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:51.930
shared set of
constitutional principles,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.610
and having those laid out
in our founding documents

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.640
and all sharing them is
increasingly something

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.540
that people don't desire or see.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.340
So let's put a pin
in that and move on

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.060
to another anniversary,
which is the 10th anniversary

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.640
of the launch of the Tea Party.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:15.220
On February 19, 2009,
CNBC editor Rick Santelli

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:17.475
was on the floor of the
Chicago Stock Exchange

00:04:17.475 --> 00:04:18.850
and wondered
whether there should

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:21.820
be something of a
Tea Party in response

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:23.650
to these various
corporate bailouts

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:25.535
that were happening
at that time.

00:04:25.535 --> 00:04:27.160
The remarks were
actually pretty brief,

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:30.460
but they struck a huge
chord with average Americans

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.620
who were fed up with
what was happening

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.520
in the federal government.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.970
This was right after the
end of the Bush presidency

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:41.980
that had ended with massive bank
bailouts and the $700 billion

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:47.410
Troubled Asset Relief Program
requiring the middle class,

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:50.260
average Americans to pay for
other people's mismanagement

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:51.700
problems.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.650
The movement grew.

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:57.790
Grassroots activists gathered
locally at the state level,

00:04:57.790 --> 00:04:59.950
repeatedly at the
national level,

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:03.310
to protest the burgeoning
administrative state

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:06.520
and its awarding of
additional power and money

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:11.260
to unelected corporations
and unelected bureaucrats.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.390
Then came the
Affordable Care Act,

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:15.820
rammed through Congress
against the fervent

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:18.610
pleas of many Americans.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:20.380
The group was
completely organic,

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.090
for better and for worse.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:27.370
It had no TV savvy leaders, it
had no public relations staff.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:29.440
Their members were very
much in the middle class,

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.750
a lot of people had
backgrounds in the military

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:34.420
and in business.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:38.530
They were a natural gift
to the Republican Party

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:40.630
because they were
just tons of people

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:44.110
arguing for what the Republican
Party claimed to support.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.710
Lowered taxes, a decrease
in the size and scope

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:51.010
of the administrative state,
an easing up or lightening

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:52.850
of the regulatory burden.

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:55.090
We're arguing for
fiscal sobriety

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:58.210
and personal responsibility.

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.820
And they were really good
at political organization.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:04.090
The movement was
responsible, in 2010,

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:07.000
for the largest shift in seats
in the House of Representatives

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.700
since 1948.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:14.260
Republicans picked up
seven Senate seats,

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:17.590
flipped control of 20
state legislatures,

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.020
and picked up another
six gubernatorial seats.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:21.755
This was really
important at a time when

00:06:21.755 --> 00:06:24.130
redistricting was happening
and other issues at the state

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:25.180
level.

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:27.940
But the response to the
group was fascinating.

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:32.470
The media couldn't or chose
not to understand the group

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:34.390
or viewed them as a
threat and immediately

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:36.850
characterized what they
were doing as racist,

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:38.680
which is their go-to slur.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.810
The Democrats understood
the party of the Tea Party

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:42.410
and fought them valiantly.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.740
But the response
from many Republicans

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:47.680
was surprisingly
hostile as well.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.620
Mitch McConnell, who is
a main player in my book

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.290
that I wrote with
Carrie Severino,

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.650
said in 2014, "I
think we're going

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:59.140
to crush them everywhere."

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:00.970
He said, referring
to the Tea Party.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.910
I don't think they're going to
have a single nominee anywhere

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:05.420
in the country.

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:07.360
And he was right,
they didn't that year.

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:09.880
He won his primary in Kentucky.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:13.060
Thad Cochran won his
primary in Mississippi,

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:18.300
and Pat Roberts won
his seat in Kansas.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.550
Other Tea Party or
Tea Party type figures

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:22.600
in that and other years
have fared better,

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:26.520
including senators Rand
Paul, Marco Rubio, Ted Cruz,

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:27.450
Ben Sasse.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:29.340
But McConnell's point stands.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.190
He said they would be
crushed, and in large part,

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:35.130
by joining with Democrats
and the media and others,

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:38.190
the establishment did
successfully crush them.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.620
They kept the insurgents
at bay, co-opted

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.330
a lot of the people that came.

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:45.780
Remember what happened with
that Affordable Care Act, people

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:47.880
who didn't like it
were told, you just

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:50.430
need to elect us to the
House of Representatives

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:51.990
and we'll take care of it.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:52.610
And they did.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:56.340
2010, we just talked about it,
that largest turnover in seats.

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.800
So the House isn't
enough, you need

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:01.020
to give us the Senate as well.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:03.570
And these people got
out there and they

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:07.440
managed to take the Senate as
well, and nothing happened.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.530
OK, OK.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:10.710
You got to give us
the presidency, too,

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.820
and then we'll really
take care of it.

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:14.070
And so they did that.

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:16.500
They, against
insurmountable odds--

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.330
seemingly insurmountable
odds, they

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:21.060
take the presidency in 2016.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:22.830
And I understand that
it's complicated.

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:25.740
I understand the Senate
Majority was very narrow.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.680
But it turned out that
they did not succeed

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:30.930
in overturning Obamacare.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:34.200
And so with that, there's
this sobering realization

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.740
that the small government
movement that was the Tea Party

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:41.780
kind of died at that moment.

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:43.752
It's also, perhaps,
worth just remembering

00:08:43.752 --> 00:08:45.710
that there was an element
of this in the Reagan

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:47.540
administration as well.

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:49.760
Many people began
to see the futility

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:53.510
of taking on the bureaucracy
and truly beating it back.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:57.230
President Reagan tried to
do this with various things,

00:08:57.230 --> 00:08:59.030
and they, by and
large, didn't work.

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:01.350
Even take reining in the EPA.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:05.360
He appointed the mother of
current Supreme Court Justice,

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:10.280
Neil Gorsuch, to deregulate,
to move regulatory control

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:13.040
to the states instead
of at the federal level.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.360
And she was pretty much run
out of town within two years.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.625
Other efforts seemed to
be met with similar ends.

00:09:20.625 --> 00:09:22.250
But with the crushing
of the Tea Party,

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:24.110
many Americans who
earnestly thought

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:26.330
that they could stand
up against the state

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:29.580
found out that they were wrong.

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:32.520
It left many Americans realizing
that the administrative state,

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:35.210
the bureaucracy, the
unelected bureaucracy,

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:38.480
could not be dismantled and that
all future political battles

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.610
might have to be
for mere survival

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:42.650
against encroaching forces.

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:45.500
Those who argued for
limited government, who

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:47.150
know that the
state can only grow

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:49.100
at the expense of the
liberty of the people,

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:51.765
learned a really tragic lesson.

00:09:51.765 --> 00:09:53.390
The notion that the
people could demand

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:56.150
that the government of
Washington DC be reduced

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:58.490
became something of a sad joke.

00:09:58.490 --> 00:10:02.000
So did much of the conservative,
or the traditional conservative

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.840
and libertarian arguments that
failed to take into account

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.710
the new power dynamics in play.

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:11.690
And one of those new dynamics
was the sudden lurch left

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:15.020
and increasing power
of corporations,

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:17.630
not content with the
mere crony capitalism

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:20.000
that they'd had before.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.580
They now are fully entrenched
within the administrative

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:24.560
state, relying on
them for their growth

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:27.500
and encouraging progressive
social change to ensure

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:29.570
compliance from the people.

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:32.630
The more embroiled and powerful
these large corporations

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:34.700
become in people's
lives, the more

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:38.060
they control the contours
of public debate.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:41.450
Understanding this treatment
of these and other voters,

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:43.010
these Tea Party
and other voters,

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:45.800
and how they have been crushed
by elected representatives

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:48.200
frittering away their
Article 1 powers

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:51.960
to the unelected bureaucracy
that serves as judge, jury,

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:54.470
and executioner,
in political life,

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:58.340
is key to making sense of this
current moment that we're in.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:00.320
It explains why so many
of the candidates that

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:03.410
were offered to voters
in the last cycle failed.

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:06.290
Now you remember
Senator John McCain

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:10.468
gave a gracious concession
speech to Barack Obama.

00:11:10.468 --> 00:11:12.260
I think that was where
he said, although it

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:15.380
might have been later, that
he viewed it as an honor

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:17.470
to lose to him.

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:18.980
And Mitt Romney's
concession speech

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:21.650
was, likewise, quite dignified.

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:25.610
And both men were known
for working very well

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:28.220
with opponents of Conservatism.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:30.210
But a lot of voters
didn't want that.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:33.080
They didn't want dignified,
beautiful concession speeches

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.710
or cooperation with
progressive leaders.

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:37.910
And they don't even
want to hear people

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:40.610
give lectures about
Conservatism that failed

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:43.910
to address this new reality.

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:46.555
They already realized there
was no taking on of the state,

00:11:46.555 --> 00:11:47.930
and they wanted
someone who would

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:50.780
help them protect their way
of life from encroachment.

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:54.050
Progressives promised to
fundamentally transform--

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:56.870
I believe that's a quote
from former President Obama--

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:59.100
America, and they
set about to do it.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.760
And leaders, a lot of
leaders in conservatism

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:04.550
don't take that
seriously enough.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:06.470
It was kind of silly
how many people

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:09.890
expected conservative voters
to just continue to accept

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:13.450
this business as usual.

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:16.750
At this point, I would like to
do something very unwise, which

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:19.570
is slightly pushback against
something Mark Steyn said,

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:21.070
so you know how
this is going to go.

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:22.580
Very poorly.

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:25.180
But the last question
that was asked last night

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:27.400
was about the media.

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:32.080
And I completely
share Mark's viewpoint

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:35.717
that freedom of the press
is extremely important.

00:12:35.717 --> 00:12:37.300
There is a reason
freedom of the press

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:40.300
is bundled with speech and
religious rights and freedom

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:41.660
of association.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:44.440
We know that we must be
free to seek the truth,

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:47.050
and the press is a very
important part of that,

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:48.610
for preserving our liberty.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:52.360
Thomas Jefferson, who wasn't
always great on things,

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:55.120
said that if he had to
choose between government

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:58.030
and newspapers, he would pick
newspapers because people

00:12:58.030 --> 00:12:59.740
need to be informed.

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:05.360
And I share a love for the
press and it's my life's work,

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:07.310
and I really wish
that we lived up

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:13.200
to what our responsibilities
are to keep people informed.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:15.730
But I don't think they're
without blame for some of what

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:18.170
has happened in recent years.

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:20.200
It is my view that
the press, the media,

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:23.270
are the primary political
actors in our midst.

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:27.230
They're openly
hostile to preserving

00:13:27.230 --> 00:13:29.240
limited constitutional
government

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:32.540
and that hostility is a threat.

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:35.240
Conservatives have complained
about media bias for decades.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:38.000
I think Eisenhower was the
first Republican president

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.040
to do so in his speeches.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:42.080
By the time of
George H. W. Bush,

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:46.970
his most popular campaign slogan
was also on a bumper sticker

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:49.940
that I remember in his
re-election campaign, which

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:55.100
was, annoy the
media, reelect Bush.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:57.410
The media response
to complaints of bias

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:59.240
has been to ignore
them or accuse

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:02.900
critics of trying to
influence the news improperly.

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:05.360
But the big response, the
main response that they have

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.580
has been simply to get worse.

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:11.120
By the 2016 campaign, you
had journalists willfully

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.640
and openly admitting that
they were not playing things

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:17.420
straight, that they
defended this posture,

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:19.250
because in their
view, Donald Trump was

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:21.450
an existential threat.

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:24.080
And when he won
the 2016 election

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:28.670
against every single media
prediction out there--

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:31.940
a few people accepted--

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:35.000
the media responded by basically
throwing temper tantrums

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:37.400
and endorsing every
crazed conspiracy

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:39.260
theory in their midst.

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:41.140
The biggest one they
bought into happened

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:43.310
to be one shared
by Hillary Clinton.

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:46.530
Hours after her election,
she gathered with advisors,

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:49.400
and they decided to lean
back into a campaign message

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:51.840
that they had worked very
hard to spread but they felt

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:54.050
had not gotten enough play.

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:55.910
It was that Trump
was illegitimate

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:58.520
because Russian
meddling in the election

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:02.240
and his treasonous
collusion with the same.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:05.720
Despite this theory
being absurd on its face,

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:07.580
the media believed
it or pretended

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:09.830
to believe it fervently.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:14.240
This crazed conspiracy theory
dominated media coverage

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:16.040
of the incoming
Trump administration

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:19.610
in the first few years
of that administration.

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:24.020
A dossier with hearsay
allegations was published.

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:27.080
And despite what most
average Americans could see,

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:31.220
it was silly, it was ridiculous,
it was difficult to believe,

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:34.640
the media treated it as if it
were credible and really worth

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:37.220
analyzing and digging into.

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:39.770
The attorney general
was sidelined

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:42.260
from oversight of the
Department of Justice

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:48.230
because of media reports
that he was a Russian spy.

00:15:48.230 --> 00:15:51.830
To this day, the notion of
Jeff Sessions from Alabama

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:54.680
being a Russian spy
is my favorite part

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:58.390
of the entire Russia
conspiracy theory.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:00.210
It caused a lot of damage.

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:01.520
A special counsel was launched.

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:04.040
And while it ran through
a great deal of money

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:06.500
and rang up many people
on process crimes,

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:08.743
the end was a complete dud.

00:16:08.743 --> 00:16:10.160
There was no
collusion with Russia

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:12.230
to steal the 2016 election.

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:15.800
Not by Trump, not by
anyone in his campaign,

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:17.233
not by a single American.

00:16:17.233 --> 00:16:18.650
Which is actually
kind of amazing.

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:20.600
You would assume that in
a country with hundreds

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:22.580
of millions of people
you got some rogue people

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:24.320
out there doing something.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:27.350
Instead, they put
forth a novel theory

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:30.680
that Trump, by
complaining about falsely

00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:33.530
being accused of
being a traitor,

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:35.910
had obstructed justice.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.820
That theory failed
to take root, too,

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:42.690
with a heavy assist from
Robert Mueller, who testified

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:45.810
on Capitol Hill, so the world
could see that he really

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:49.260
did not have control over the
probe whose credibility rested

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:50.640
on his name.

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:52.590
I remember that day
that he testified,

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:56.352
my mother told me that she had
not read the Mueller Report,

00:16:56.352 --> 00:16:58.560
but she was pretty sure she
had a better handle on it

00:16:58.560 --> 00:16:59.620
than Robert Mueller did.

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:07.630
We also might take a
minute to note media

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:10.170
coverage of another story
I'm very familiar with,

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:13.390
which is the Kavanaugh
confirmation battle.

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:16.240
The president nominated
a respected federal judge

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:18.640
for the court, and
the media immediately

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:22.000
set about fighting him,
fighting against the nomination.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.000
They end up publicizing
lurid allegations

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:28.128
without any supporting
evidence whatsoever.

00:17:28.128 --> 00:17:29.920
This really is a break
from how-- you know,

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.290
I know we complain about the
media, or people in America

00:17:32.290 --> 00:17:33.910
complain about the media a lot.

00:17:33.910 --> 00:17:35.860
But even a few decades
ago, you would have not

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:39.340
seen quite this glee
with publicizing

00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:42.730
these lurid allegations without
any supporting evidence.

00:17:42.730 --> 00:17:44.200
Most recently,
The New York Times

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:47.500
tried to hit him again
with another smear

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:49.870
by failing to tell readers
that they-- well, they

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:51.940
said they had an allegation
against him, which

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:56.440
was a physically improbable and
somewhat ridiculous allegation.

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:59.650
They neglected to tell readers
that the alleged victim had

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:03.270
never said that
she was a victim,

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:05.020
and she had not spoken
to these reporters,

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:07.100
but she had said through
multiple friends,

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:10.000
she has no such recollection
of any incident.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.400
They kept that from readers.

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:15.730
The trail of failure associated
with the media in recent years

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:18.190
is breathtaking.

00:18:18.190 --> 00:18:20.960
There was the smear of the
pro-life teens from Covington,

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:26.570
Kentucky who were falsely
accused of racial intimidation.

00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:29.810
Grown journalists
grilling 16-year-old boys

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:32.120
about their behavior when
it was the journalists who

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:34.010
got everything wrong.

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:36.030
There are so many stories
that are debunked,

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:39.620
it's basically impossible
to tally them all.

00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:42.890
The media are not tiring,
they have big plans.

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:46.550
They obviously now have
a huge impeachment plan,

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:50.270
and it can be viewed that they
are running the impeachment

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:55.100
effort more than the Democratic
politicians themselves.

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:56.720
The New York Times
recently announced

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:58.730
that the implosion of
the Russia narrative

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:01.220
had left their newsroom
reeling and that they

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:03.260
were trying to
rebuild their newsroom

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:05.392
around racial narratives.

00:19:05.392 --> 00:19:07.100
Interesting, first
off to note, that they

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:11.240
built their entire newsroom
around pushing this conspiracy

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:14.420
theory that anyone with
even moderate intelligence

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:17.645
could have seen reason
to be skeptical about.

00:19:17.645 --> 00:19:21.200
And when it imploded, the editor
said, that really hit us hard.

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:23.600
And now we're trying
to rebuild by claiming

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.880
that our political
opponents are all racist.

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:27.740
And they said this in
a transcript that was

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:29.700
leaked that everybody can read.

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:34.910
But as part of that campaign,
they launched the 1619 Project

00:19:34.910 --> 00:19:39.290
to mark the 400th anniversary
of slavery in the United States.

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:41.690
The project makes Al
Gore's misstatement

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:44.370
of the American
motto look quaint.

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:47.840
It's not just out of one,
many, but a reformulation

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:51.650
of what the country is founded
upon to explicitly reject

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:54.470
our founding documents
and their meaning.

00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:57.830
Now it is perfectly fine and
even good for The New York

00:19:57.830 --> 00:20:01.640
Times and all of America to
mark the 400th anniversary

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:03.470
of slavery in this country.

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:06.350
It's a somber and
important historical marker

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:09.290
and reminder of what
humans are capable of doing

00:20:09.290 --> 00:20:10.890
against each other.

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:12.880
That's not what
The Times is doing.

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:16.760
It went so far as to say that
this 400th anniversary is

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:20.570
the true founding of the
country, not 1776, when

00:20:20.570 --> 00:20:22.670
our forefathers risked
their lives to say all men

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:24.180
were created equal.

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:27.050
The lead essay says that
all of that rhetoric

00:20:27.050 --> 00:20:29.960
was meaningless because the real
reason for the Revolutionary

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:34.790
War was to preserve
slavery in America.

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:39.020
Never mind that slavery had
been in America for 150 years

00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:41.570
under the rule of
an English King

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:45.213
or that slavery did not start
out as an American institution.

00:20:45.213 --> 00:20:46.880
There's a huge
difference between saying

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:48.422
the country was
founded on the belief

00:20:48.422 --> 00:20:51.650
that all men were created equal,
and that it took us far too

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:57.410
long to realize that
ideal, and saying it was

00:20:57.410 --> 00:20:59.600
disingenuous from the start.

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:03.290
Not to mention how many
Americans gave their lives

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:04.970
in the Civil War to
make that promise

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:07.580
in the Declaration a reality.

00:21:07.580 --> 00:21:11.930
We were led into that war, or
we had to respond to that war

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:15.830
that we were forced into,
by a man who explicitly made

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:17.430
that argument to
the American people

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:19.310
in speeches and
official statements,

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:22.897
that this was what we owed
because of our founding.

00:21:22.897 --> 00:21:24.980
From the earliest times,
there were many Americans

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:26.630
between the founding
in the Civil War

00:21:26.630 --> 00:21:28.520
that were dedicated
to fulfilling

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:30.770
the promise of our creeds.

00:21:30.770 --> 00:21:32.730
Chief among them,
for our purposes,

00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:35.600
we're the founders of
Hillsdale, abolitionists

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:38.000
was the first American
college to prohibit,

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:40.100
in its charter, any
discrimination based

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:43.370
on race, religion, or sex,
and became an early force

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:45.270
for the abolition of slavery.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:47.840
A higher percentage
of Hillsdale students

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:52.640
enlisted in the Civil War than
any other Western College.

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:55.670
Of the more than 400 who
fought to preserve the Union,

00:21:55.670 --> 00:21:58.160
four won the Congressional
Medal of Honor,

00:21:58.160 --> 00:22:00.620
three became generals,
and many more served

00:22:00.620 --> 00:22:02.270
as regimental commanders.

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:06.260
60 gave their lives.

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:08.720
That commitment leads to today.

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:14.550
And it is in stark
contrast to what we

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:16.470
see from a lot of other people.

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:19.560
I have a little discursion
here, if you don't mind.

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:21.030
Where I've been
thinking, it's been

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:24.450
such a wonderful experience
to hear some great minds

00:22:24.450 --> 00:22:27.810
speak to us these
last couple of days.

00:22:27.810 --> 00:22:32.280
And it reminds me of just
how different Hillsdale

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:35.280
is from so many other
institutions that are

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:37.500
in the Conservative movement.

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:43.590
I'll put it this way.

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:50.010
I'm reminded of how Vladimir
Putin controls his opposition.

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:53.820
He has a tight grip on power
in Russia, and one of the ways

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:57.420
that he does that is by
allowing what is called

00:22:57.420 --> 00:22:59.760
the systematic opposition.

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:01.410
And the systematic
opposition are

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:03.930
people who claim to
oppose him, but are

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:06.360
given some seats of power.

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:08.580
And he genuinely listens
to them to make sure

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:13.620
that he doesn't go so far as
to lose control of his power.

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:17.280
Technically, they are in
the opposing party from his,

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:19.710
but they're generally
fine with a continuation

00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:22.500
of his government,
so long as they

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:25.710
get to keep their
power and their money.

00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:29.350
They serve a useful
purpose for Putin.

00:23:29.350 --> 00:23:31.710
And I think for too
long, too many people

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:34.920
in the Conservative
movement in DC, including

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:38.430
too many people in its media
outlets, academic institutions,

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:42.030
think tanks and
otherwise, have functioned

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:46.200
as the systematic opposition
to Progressivism's march

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:49.620
through American institutions,
public and private.

00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:52.620
Technically they were opposed,
and they'd make sounds

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:54.840
about opposing the growth
of the administrative state

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:56.370
and the broad of the culture.

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:58.950
But they were never terribly
successful at returning

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:00.720
the country to its
first principles

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:03.570
or constitutional order,
despite the millions

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:05.550
of supporters who
put them in power

00:24:05.550 --> 00:24:11.012
and expected just that, expected
not just rhetoric but results.

00:24:11.012 --> 00:24:13.220
I'm supposed to be talking
about the political divide

00:24:13.220 --> 00:24:16.040
in America, but it seems to
me that this isn't really

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:17.540
a political divide,
all these things

00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:20.180
that we're talking about,
but a cultural divide.

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:23.250
One side believes we are
created with purpose,

00:24:23.250 --> 00:24:25.320
we seek truth in
order to do good,

00:24:25.320 --> 00:24:29.210
we understand human nature and
how it is shared by all of us.

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:31.380
Our laws come from
these beliefs,

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:33.590
and the Founders wrote them
into our documents that

00:24:33.590 --> 00:24:35.660
form the basis of our republic.

00:24:35.660 --> 00:24:38.870
Another side embraces
a fervent religiosity,

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:41.900
a secular religiosity
built around the exercise

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:44.135
of raw majoritarian power.

00:24:44.135 --> 00:24:46.550
In many cases they
reject reality,

00:24:46.550 --> 00:24:49.640
even if it means the reality
of biological distinctions

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:51.350
between male and female.

00:24:51.350 --> 00:24:53.630
They reject the very
notion of a divine creator

00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:57.110
who sets forth rights and
wrongs through space and time.

00:24:57.110 --> 00:24:59.510
And relatedly,
there is a rejection

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:01.610
of redemption and
forgiveness leading

00:25:01.610 --> 00:25:05.900
to brutal ritual punishments
and mobs tearing down neighbors.

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:09.570
Perhaps goes without saying that
along with The New York Times,

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:12.840
they reject our current
form of government as well.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:14.270
It's not a political divide.

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.070
It is so much more than that.

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:17.570
And anyone who
thinks that this will

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:20.150
be resolved through an
election or something like that

00:25:20.150 --> 00:25:21.980
is not thinking clearly.

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:24.140
These pressures and divides
have been building up

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:25.740
for more than a century.

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:28.040
Just as Roe v Wade
didn't magically

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:30.920
end the debate over
human rights or abortion,

00:25:30.920 --> 00:25:34.400
a single election, even
a series of elections

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:38.000
is not going to bridge the gap
between two dominant cultures.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:40.940
Hillsdale College, under
its very able leadership,

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:43.400
understands the
seriousness of the problem

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:46.250
and the insufficiency of many
of the current approaches

00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:49.760
to tackling American problems.

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:51.830
We don't need a
rear guard action

00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:55.880
tisk tisking at the media
for being historically wrong.

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:58.880
We need a lot of people,
the kind of people

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:01.460
who have the education
that Hillsdale provides,

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:04.160
who fully understand these
documents and these beliefs

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:06.890
that define us as
Americans, so that we

00:26:06.890 --> 00:26:09.860
can do intellectual
battle to fight and win.

00:26:09.860 --> 00:26:12.260
And not to simply
conserve some tiny piece

00:26:12.260 --> 00:26:13.940
of what we used to have.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:17.670
As our country's divides become
greater and more worrisome,

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:20.210
we need more Americans
who understand

00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:23.570
the history of our founding, the
philosophies that undergird it,

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:26.530
and what divided government
seeks to accomplish.

00:26:26.530 --> 00:26:29.630
This is being done in
the context of a movement

00:26:29.630 --> 00:26:32.600
in this country to
erase our history,

00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:35.240
in progressive madrassas,
essentially, that

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:36.740
reimagined these things.

00:26:36.740 --> 00:26:39.050
With a media that seeks
obfuscation rather than

00:26:39.050 --> 00:26:40.160
enlightenment.

00:26:40.160 --> 00:26:42.230
The main thing that
gives me hope right now

00:26:42.230 --> 00:26:45.410
is classical liberal
arts education.

00:26:45.410 --> 00:26:49.730
My children attend a school,
a classical Lutheran school,

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:51.680
that is staffed by
Hillsdale graduates.

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:54.320
That is the number one thing
I'm happy about in my life

00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:55.270
right now.

00:26:55.270 --> 00:26:58.090
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:04.210
It is unclear, frankly,
if the republic

00:27:04.210 --> 00:27:06.400
will survive the
progressive onslaught being

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:08.290
waged against it right now.

00:27:08.290 --> 00:27:11.020
But if our founding
ideals are to live on,

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:14.800
it will be through the students
educated here, inculcated

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:18.640
with virtuous character, refined
through challenging study,

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:20.900
and sent forth with purpose.

00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:21.641
Thank you.

00:27:21.641 --> 00:27:27.042
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:43.670
And in general, I prefer
to answer questions.

00:27:43.670 --> 00:27:45.260
So we have time
for some questions.

00:27:45.260 --> 00:27:47.260
SPEAKER 2: So thank
you Mrs. Hemingway.

00:27:47.260 --> 00:27:49.660
We do now have time
for Q&amp;A, please

00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:51.970
make your way to the microphone
if you have a question.

00:27:57.288 --> 00:27:59.330
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: Or I
can just ramble more, too.

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:06.130
AUDIENCE: I was the one that
asked that question of Mark

00:28:06.130 --> 00:28:07.540
last evening.

00:28:07.540 --> 00:28:12.830
And I'm very troubled by
what's going on in the media.

00:28:12.830 --> 00:28:16.660
I guess my question to
you would be, how do we

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:21.220
battle the media being
a cabal with the Left

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:25.450
and the Progressives when
there's no punishment for them

00:28:25.450 --> 00:28:30.670
either making it up or twisting
it to favor what they're doing?

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:33.100
I understand, like
I said last night,

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:35.410
Sullivan v. New York
Times, but there's

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:39.730
got to be a way that someone
can hold the press accountable.

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:43.510
If somebody falls on
my property in Florida,

00:28:43.510 --> 00:28:46.420
they can sue me if
I've been neglectful.

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:49.360
My gosh, some of
these reporters,

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:52.540
they'd have to be blind not
to uncover some of the stuff

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:54.620
that they should
be talking about.

00:28:54.620 --> 00:28:56.203
So I'm sorry for
taking up time, but--

00:28:56.203 --> 00:28:58.703
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: No that's my
favorite thing to talk about.

00:28:58.703 --> 00:29:00.670
So I would like--
yes, to add to this.

00:29:00.670 --> 00:29:03.160
By the way, did you know
that Justice Thomas had

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:06.910
a little note this last
year where he mentioned

00:29:06.910 --> 00:29:11.380
that it might be time to revisit
New York Times versus Sullivan.

00:29:11.380 --> 00:29:12.010
Yes.

00:29:12.010 --> 00:29:16.150
So the issue there
is that I actually

00:29:16.150 --> 00:29:18.820
think the press might be the
biggest threat to press freedom

00:29:18.820 --> 00:29:19.690
right now.

00:29:19.690 --> 00:29:24.580
And you saw, in recent years,
you saw that media inability

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:26.950
to protect people's
privacy actually led

00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:29.800
to some judgments against
prominent media figures.

00:29:29.800 --> 00:29:34.450
There was the Gawker case where
Hulk Hogan sued them and won

00:29:34.450 --> 00:29:38.080
and destroyed Gawker
because of their willingness

00:29:38.080 --> 00:29:41.710
to just publish something that
had no news value that really

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:43.060
harmed his life.

00:29:43.060 --> 00:29:47.650
And it was the failure of
that media outlet itself

00:29:47.650 --> 00:29:49.840
and other media outlets
that soften the ground

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:53.750
to enable that kind of win
against a media company.

00:29:53.750 --> 00:29:57.820
And I think you're seeing that
with this willful, deliberate,

00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:02.650
malicious behavior
by some media folks.

00:30:02.650 --> 00:30:06.060
The principles in
journalism that we have,

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:07.860
that we're taught,
should have been

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:10.410
enough to prevent what
happened in Covington.

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:11.500
And they weren't.

00:30:11.500 --> 00:30:15.480
And I'm not saying I
think he necessarily,

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:19.390
that this kid who's sued
everybody under the sun--

00:30:19.390 --> 00:30:22.020
rah, go-- that he'll win.

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:26.880
But he has, I think, a
solid case to get something.

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:30.320
They tried to destroy him and
they had no reason to do it.

00:30:30.320 --> 00:30:32.800
They didn't gather facts
before they did it.

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:36.780
And there are some legal
vulnerabilities that they have.

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:39.690
But more than that, I think
it's important for people

00:30:39.690 --> 00:30:45.330
to not ascribe to the media
a cultural power that they

00:30:45.330 --> 00:30:46.680
no longer have.

00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:51.420
And this is something where we
have generally regarded them

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:54.930
as having the right to set
conversations, to dictate

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:56.100
what conversations will be.

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:58.860
They get to moderate
presidential debates.

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:01.980
And that power was given
to them on the assumption

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:04.440
that they would take that
responsibility seriously

00:31:04.440 --> 00:31:05.760
and that they would be fair.

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:08.290
Well, they're not, and they
haven't been for a long time.

00:31:08.290 --> 00:31:11.550
So people need to start
thinking about other ways to be.

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:13.140
The Democratic
National Committee

00:31:13.140 --> 00:31:16.020
announced that it wouldn't
do anything with Fox News

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:18.330
because unlike the
other media outlets,

00:31:18.330 --> 00:31:21.497
they might get gentle
pushback at Fox News.

00:31:21.497 --> 00:31:23.580
And it was interesting to
see how people responded

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:27.360
to it, including, I think, a lot
of people at Fox were saying,

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:29.730
accurately, we're
fair, we should not

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:32.730
be punished for being fair just
because other media outlets

00:31:32.730 --> 00:31:35.040
are so crazy on the left.

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:37.170
But it is kind of weird
why Republicans just

00:31:37.170 --> 00:31:39.542
agree to go on these media
outlets that are extensions

00:31:39.542 --> 00:31:41.250
of the Democratic
Party that are actually

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:42.578
leading the Democratic Party.

00:31:42.578 --> 00:31:44.370
It seems like maybe
they should think about

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:46.050
whether that's
working out for them,

00:31:46.050 --> 00:31:48.910
or what happens when, to
get to a point of truth,

00:31:48.910 --> 00:31:51.660
you have to go through so
many of the underlying false

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:54.102
assumptions that undergird
each question that it's not

00:31:54.102 --> 00:31:56.310
worth your time or that you
won't get fair treatment.

00:31:56.310 --> 00:31:58.568
So anyway, yes there are
some legal opportunities.

00:31:58.568 --> 00:32:00.360
But more than anything,
I think people just

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:05.430
need to stop treating them as
if they have a cultural power

00:32:05.430 --> 00:32:06.500
that they no longer have.

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:10.420
AUDIENCE: Hello.

00:32:10.420 --> 00:32:14.080
First of all, I'd like to say
I think of you and Kimberley

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:17.072
Strassel as the dynamic duo.

00:32:17.072 --> 00:32:18.030
And we are so grateful.

00:32:18.030 --> 00:32:20.265
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:21.610 --> 00:32:24.120
And I just want
to encourage you.

00:32:24.120 --> 00:32:26.880
I'm sure it's not easy.

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:28.840
Two quick questions for you.

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:32.280
One is the term progressive,
to me is confusing,

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:37.190
because it's been also
applied to President Trump.

00:32:37.190 --> 00:32:40.500
And so how can the
Progressive's be against Trump

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:42.810
if Trump was also progressive.

00:32:42.810 --> 00:32:46.620
So there's some confusion
about terminology in my mind.

00:32:46.620 --> 00:32:51.030
And then the other thing that's
even more of interest to me

00:32:51.030 --> 00:32:55.320
right now is what
would your forecast

00:32:55.320 --> 00:33:00.052
be for this impeachment
process, as you see it going--

00:33:00.052 --> 00:33:01.260
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: Is that it?

00:33:01.260 --> 00:33:02.790
Just those three easy.

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:05.430
I'll just discard
them right away.

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:08.190
First off, I just want to say,
I use the term progressive

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:10.710
to refer to the Progressive
movement, this more

00:33:10.710 --> 00:33:13.650
than 100-year-old
movement that really

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:15.210
got going with Woodrow Wilson.

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:18.930
That has this idea that
human nature can be conquered

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:21.060
and that the Constitution
is a barrier that

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:22.092
needs to be overcome.

00:33:22.092 --> 00:33:23.550
I don't like using
the word liberal

00:33:23.550 --> 00:33:25.440
because I think liberal
is a great word,

00:33:25.440 --> 00:33:28.740
and so I'm a little
stubborn on that.

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:31.710
But progressive also
sounds like a nice word.

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:33.880
Oh, we want to progress,
we want to do well.

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:35.970
But the actual
movement is very evil

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:38.610
and has a lot of bad,
bad baggage that it

00:33:38.610 --> 00:33:41.460
should be held accountable for.

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:43.390
I'll do impeachment but
just really quickly,

00:33:43.390 --> 00:33:47.130
the first thing you said about
it being difficult to speak

00:33:47.130 --> 00:33:49.530
against the hive mind.

00:33:49.530 --> 00:33:52.650
I am not saying that
it's always easy,

00:33:52.650 --> 00:33:55.740
but I get very
frustrated when people--

00:33:55.740 --> 00:34:00.430
I hear a lot of people say, oh,
this moment is so exhausting.

00:34:00.430 --> 00:34:03.290
And they say, oh, just
Trump is so exhausting.

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:05.620
I'm just exhausted
by everything.

00:34:05.620 --> 00:34:07.620
And you hear a lot of
conservative pundits

00:34:07.620 --> 00:34:10.139
say this or Republican leaders.

00:34:10.139 --> 00:34:11.710
Oh, we have to do this again.

00:34:11.710 --> 00:34:15.449
And I keep thinking, it is
such an easy job, compared

00:34:15.449 --> 00:34:18.120
to most jobs, to get
to write for a living

00:34:18.120 --> 00:34:20.902
or go on TV for a few
minutes, or do a radio

00:34:20.902 --> 00:34:22.110
show, or something like that.

00:34:22.110 --> 00:34:24.270
That's relatively
easy compared to what

00:34:24.270 --> 00:34:25.800
everybody else is doing.

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:27.989
And that you get to do
it is not exhausting.

00:34:27.989 --> 00:34:30.300
And if you find it
exhausting, you probably

00:34:30.300 --> 00:34:32.215
shouldn't do it
anymore, because there

00:34:32.215 --> 00:34:34.590
are a lot of people who are
counting on you to articulate

00:34:34.590 --> 00:34:37.929
conservative principles or to
advocate for different things.

00:34:37.929 --> 00:34:42.889
And if you can't muster that
energy, other people can.

00:34:42.889 --> 00:34:43.889
So just head on out.

00:34:43.889 --> 00:34:45.739
But anyway, that's
a little sideline.

00:34:45.739 --> 00:34:46.755
As for the impeachment--

00:34:46.755 --> 00:34:50.510
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:50.510 --> 00:34:52.250
The impeachment
thing, I predicted

00:34:52.250 --> 00:34:54.333
he would be impeached
already, so I'm a little bit

00:34:54.333 --> 00:34:56.610
behind schedule on that.

00:34:56.610 --> 00:34:58.070
And I don't really know--

00:34:58.070 --> 00:34:59.290
I would have just assumed--

00:34:59.290 --> 00:35:00.850
like I said, I thought it
would have happened already.

00:35:00.850 --> 00:35:02.420
So when this started I
thought, well, of course

00:35:02.420 --> 00:35:03.390
it's going to happen.

00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:06.110
I take such extreme
delight in how

00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:07.880
we're still trying
to figure out what

00:35:07.880 --> 00:35:09.290
we're going to impeach him for.

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:11.370
Like the impeachment is
a foregone conclusion,

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:14.540
but like well, what
can we find here?

00:35:14.540 --> 00:35:17.780
Which is not how it should go.

00:35:17.780 --> 00:35:19.790
And I still assume
that they will.

00:35:19.790 --> 00:35:23.240
I will say, this rollout is
going much more poorly for them

00:35:23.240 --> 00:35:25.430
than all of their
other attempts.

00:35:25.430 --> 00:35:29.360
And that might actually prevent
impeachment from happening.

00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:32.750
And when the Russia
hoax started,

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:35.030
you saw a lot of
people in Washington DC

00:35:35.030 --> 00:35:37.820
take it seriously on
both sides of the aisle.

00:35:37.820 --> 00:35:40.760
You almost saw people
hoping it were true as a way

00:35:40.760 --> 00:35:43.220
to get rid of him, and you saw
a lot of Republican leaders

00:35:43.220 --> 00:35:45.560
and political pundits and
whatnot say, I don't know,

00:35:45.560 --> 00:35:47.360
this sounds really serious.

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:50.750
And that enabled
things to get crazy.

00:35:50.750 --> 00:35:54.152
And it gave the people running
this operation a lot of power.

00:35:54.152 --> 00:35:55.610
And with this thing,
I just thought

00:35:55.610 --> 00:35:59.090
it was interesting that right
away people were like, eh,

00:35:59.090 --> 00:36:00.920
doesn't sound like much.

00:36:00.920 --> 00:36:04.610
Because they've already been
through the Kavanaugh thing,

00:36:04.610 --> 00:36:07.555
they've already been
through the Russia thing.

00:36:07.555 --> 00:36:08.930
And all the other
cultural things

00:36:08.930 --> 00:36:10.970
like the Covington boys and
everything else like that,

00:36:10.970 --> 00:36:13.637
that they're not willing to just
accept what the media claim are

00:36:13.637 --> 00:36:14.330
big issues.

00:36:14.330 --> 00:36:16.610
Not to mention that every
piece of actual information

00:36:16.610 --> 00:36:19.450
you get, just like with Russia,
they'll promise a bombshell

00:36:19.450 --> 00:36:20.825
and then when you
read it, you're

00:36:20.825 --> 00:36:22.530
like eh, that doesn't
sound like much.

00:36:22.530 --> 00:36:24.740
And so what I'm
mostly interested in

00:36:24.740 --> 00:36:27.720
is how Republicans don't seem
to be playing along with it.

00:36:27.720 --> 00:36:29.683
That's surprising to me.

00:36:29.683 --> 00:36:31.100
They seem to be
pushing back hard.

00:36:31.100 --> 00:36:33.380
You have people
attempting to censor

00:36:33.380 --> 00:36:35.450
Adam Schiff for colluding
with the whistleblower

00:36:35.450 --> 00:36:40.160
before the whistle was blown,
which is just so fishy.

00:36:40.160 --> 00:36:41.300
And he is a fishy person.

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:43.220
And this is a part
of a pattern for him

00:36:43.220 --> 00:36:44.832
of saying things that are false.

00:36:44.832 --> 00:36:46.790
You have people in the
Senate pushing back hard

00:36:46.790 --> 00:36:50.180
against the idea that it is
not legitimate to ask Ukraine

00:36:50.180 --> 00:36:52.700
to explain why it meddled
with Hillary Clinton

00:36:52.700 --> 00:36:54.070
in our 2016 election.

00:36:54.070 --> 00:36:55.598
So it might not go
as well for them.

00:36:55.598 --> 00:36:56.140
I don't know.

00:36:58.670 --> 00:37:01.000
AUDIENCE: I'll start by
echoing the compliment

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:04.310
about how good a fighter you
are for what we believe in.

00:37:04.310 --> 00:37:05.200
Thank you for that.

00:37:05.200 --> 00:37:06.242
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: Thanks.

00:37:06.242 --> 00:37:08.830
AUDIENCE: You talked about
the passion and the sacrifice

00:37:08.830 --> 00:37:11.350
and the commitment of
the founding generation.

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:16.830
So any thoughts, suggestions, on
how to make liberty passionate

00:37:16.830 --> 00:37:17.330
again?

00:37:19.948 --> 00:37:21.490
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY:
It's a big question

00:37:21.490 --> 00:37:26.370
that I'm not sure I have enough
of a good thing to respond to.

00:37:26.370 --> 00:37:28.000
But I am hopeful.

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:29.740
I'm always kind of hopeful.

00:37:29.740 --> 00:37:33.550
Part of it is that
there really are

00:37:33.550 --> 00:37:36.700
some people who seem to
be willing to stand up

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:38.260
in difficult scenarios.

00:37:38.260 --> 00:37:41.210
Part of it is that as
things get more oppressive,

00:37:41.210 --> 00:37:44.260
I think it makes choices
more clear for people.

00:37:44.260 --> 00:37:48.760
We are really just living
in the fruition of decades

00:37:48.760 --> 00:37:51.040
of progressive
education and what

00:37:51.040 --> 00:37:52.770
it has done to a lot of people.

00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:54.520
But there are a lot
of people who were not

00:37:54.520 --> 00:37:57.520
educated that way
and who do love

00:37:57.520 --> 00:37:58.930
what we have here,
and understand

00:37:58.930 --> 00:38:00.790
that it needs to be fought for.

00:38:00.790 --> 00:38:04.180
I wonder if we're too
comfortable to actually have

00:38:04.180 --> 00:38:07.750
the fight that we need to
have, and that is a risk.

00:38:07.750 --> 00:38:10.420
But you don't pray that things
get worse so that people

00:38:10.420 --> 00:38:12.190
aren't uncomfortable.

00:38:12.190 --> 00:38:13.890
But I don't know.

00:38:13.890 --> 00:38:18.160
I think, again, it's
perhaps a small thing.

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:22.510
But speaking about why we're
here, I do a lot of hiring

00:38:22.510 --> 00:38:24.940
and I meet a lot
of new graduates.

00:38:24.940 --> 00:38:28.300
And a lot of them do not
inspire confidence in me

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:29.680
or give me hope.

00:38:29.680 --> 00:38:32.920
But the success rate of working
with Hillsdale graduates

00:38:32.920 --> 00:38:35.000
has been just unbelievable.

00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:37.870
They seem to be able to think
critically, write clearly,

00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:40.660
understand the significance
of what they're talking about.

00:38:40.660 --> 00:38:44.564
You do that enough and you
start having a good movement.

00:38:44.564 --> 00:38:48.956
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:51.400 --> 00:38:55.240
AUDIENCE: How instructive is it
for the future of United States

00:38:55.240 --> 00:38:58.990
democracy with what's
happening in Hong Kong

00:38:58.990 --> 00:39:02.500
and with some of the protesters
carrying American flags?

00:39:06.080 --> 00:39:09.020
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: Could you
explain that a little bit more?

00:39:09.020 --> 00:39:10.673
What you're asking, sorry.

00:39:10.673 --> 00:39:12.590
AUDIENCE: What effect
this might have on China

00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:15.800
itself, what effect
this might well

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:20.328
have on our perception of
democracy around the world?

00:39:20.328 --> 00:39:21.620
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: I don't know.

00:39:21.620 --> 00:39:23.930
I think that,
unfortunately, a lot of this

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:27.920
was already lost by allowing
Hong Kong to be handed over,

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:31.100
and they already were on
a pretty tight timeline.

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:33.780
But we were thinking
another 30 years,

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:35.900
and it might be much
shorter than that

00:39:35.900 --> 00:39:38.617
before their oppression comes.

00:39:38.617 --> 00:39:40.700
I actually have friends
who are missionaries there

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:43.132
who were talking about
what they've been focused

00:39:43.132 --> 00:39:45.590
on since they got there was
just the limited time that they

00:39:45.590 --> 00:39:48.290
had before China takes over.

00:39:48.290 --> 00:39:49.560
Really takes over.

00:39:49.560 --> 00:39:53.090
But realizing that it might be
even shorter is very harrowing.

00:39:53.090 --> 00:39:54.890
On the other hand,
it is so inspiring

00:39:54.890 --> 00:39:57.590
to see that they understand
what they're fighting for

00:39:57.590 --> 00:39:59.360
and why they're fighting for it.

00:39:59.360 --> 00:40:01.400
And it does give you
this glimmer of hope.

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:03.710
You wish that we could do
something to help out with

00:40:03.710 --> 00:40:08.960
that, which we really cannot do
much right now without making

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:10.895
the situation much
worse, probably.

00:40:10.895 --> 00:40:12.770
But I think one thing
that's been interesting

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:15.270
about the Trump administration
is how much the attention has

00:40:15.270 --> 00:40:18.710
turned to China, as it should
have been for a long time.

00:40:18.710 --> 00:40:20.210
And even our battle
with North Korea

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:23.000
is best understood as a
recognition of China's growing

00:40:23.000 --> 00:40:23.962
power.

00:40:23.962 --> 00:40:25.670
They've been so smart
and savvy about how

00:40:25.670 --> 00:40:28.370
they are building and
accruing power and influence

00:40:28.370 --> 00:40:30.260
across the globe.

00:40:30.260 --> 00:40:33.230
But if we lose that
stronghold, that's bad.

00:40:33.230 --> 00:40:36.323
One great thing, the
financial markets there, China

00:40:36.323 --> 00:40:37.490
might not want to mess with.

00:40:37.490 --> 00:40:40.758
And that might give the
people there some leeway

00:40:40.758 --> 00:40:42.050
to continue with their efforts.

00:40:48.030 --> 00:40:48.780
AUDIENCE: Mollie--

00:40:48.780 --> 00:40:49.697
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: Yes.

00:40:49.697 --> 00:40:51.760
AUDIENCE: I'm still
stuck back on Benghazi.

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:57.220
I just found it incredible.

00:40:57.220 --> 00:41:00.520
I come from the home
district of Trey Gowdy,

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:04.600
and I thought for sure you'd
have her dead to rights on that

00:41:04.600 --> 00:41:05.260
one.

00:41:05.260 --> 00:41:07.540
And nothing happened.

00:41:07.540 --> 00:41:09.190
So if you could
speak to Benghazi.

00:41:09.190 --> 00:41:12.520
But the other thing that
I'm really excited to know

00:41:12.520 --> 00:41:15.910
is who writes the democratic
talking points that come out

00:41:15.910 --> 00:41:19.090
every morning and it
says the same thing

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:21.220
all across the media spectrum.

00:41:21.220 --> 00:41:22.515
Who does that?

00:41:22.515 --> 00:41:24.640
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: I mean,
it actually does probably

00:41:24.640 --> 00:41:26.260
come out as talking points.

00:41:26.260 --> 00:41:28.100
You really do get--
when you're on TV

00:41:28.100 --> 00:41:29.620
or when you're in
communications,

00:41:29.620 --> 00:41:31.382
people just send
you what they think

00:41:31.382 --> 00:41:32.590
the talking points should be.

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:34.570
It is entirely possible
that they are just

00:41:34.570 --> 00:41:36.880
getting the same shared,
what it sounds like,

00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:38.453
might be exactly
what's happening.

00:41:38.453 --> 00:41:40.120
But it's also true
that people sometimes

00:41:40.120 --> 00:41:42.400
don't need talking points
because they already

00:41:42.400 --> 00:41:44.540
agree with the agenda.

00:41:44.540 --> 00:41:46.570
And so they just kind
of know what to say.

00:41:46.570 --> 00:41:49.990
Yesterday, it came out
that Adam Schiff had worked

00:41:49.990 --> 00:41:51.903
with this whistleblower
before it came out,

00:41:51.903 --> 00:41:53.320
which a lot of
people were saying,

00:41:53.320 --> 00:41:56.290
it seems like Adam Schiff knows
a lot about this whistleblower

00:41:56.290 --> 00:41:57.580
before it came out.

00:41:57.580 --> 00:41:59.860
And the media said,
that's a conspiracy theory

00:41:59.860 --> 00:42:01.160
and you should stop saying it.

00:42:01.160 --> 00:42:03.310
And it turns out the
people who were saying

00:42:03.310 --> 00:42:04.930
that we're absolutely right.

00:42:04.930 --> 00:42:08.260
The New York Times spun it in
the nicest possible fashion.

00:42:08.260 --> 00:42:10.660
They were like,
OK, Adam Schiff was

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:14.350
working with the whistleblower,
in a clearly partisan action

00:42:14.350 --> 00:42:15.850
designed to get impeachment.

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:18.190
But don't worry, it's
all on the up and up.

00:42:18.190 --> 00:42:22.092
And you saw all the media
people just kind of parrot this.

00:42:22.092 --> 00:42:23.050
Nothing to worry about.

00:42:23.050 --> 00:42:24.760
Yes, Adam Schiff
lied to me personally

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:26.385
when he said he hadn't
talked with him,

00:42:26.385 --> 00:42:27.860
but nothing to worry about.

00:42:27.860 --> 00:42:31.180
But you saw other
people rejecting that,

00:42:31.180 --> 00:42:32.290
and that made me happy.

00:42:32.290 --> 00:42:34.082
About the Benghazi
thing, one of the things

00:42:34.082 --> 00:42:36.790
I've learned by covering these
really complex situations

00:42:36.790 --> 00:42:40.720
is just how effectively
many people have weaponized

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:44.590
complexity, and
also how difficult

00:42:44.590 --> 00:42:46.390
it is to hold people
accountable when

00:42:46.390 --> 00:42:50.140
you don't have a lot of
key people helping out.

00:42:50.140 --> 00:42:52.420
You can control
Congress, but you

00:42:52.420 --> 00:42:55.810
don't have control of
the Department of Justice

00:42:55.810 --> 00:42:58.360
where you might be able to
encourage something to happen

00:42:58.360 --> 00:42:59.620
or what not.

00:42:59.620 --> 00:43:03.010
The Benghazi report
that came out just

00:43:03.010 --> 00:43:06.010
showed that a lot of people
who had questions about it

00:43:06.010 --> 00:43:07.252
were right.

00:43:07.252 --> 00:43:08.710
And there's so much
more that would

00:43:08.710 --> 00:43:10.450
be interesting to
look into there

00:43:10.450 --> 00:43:12.580
about why we were in Libya
in the first place, who

00:43:12.580 --> 00:43:15.290
had personal financial
interests there and whatnot.

00:43:15.290 --> 00:43:18.650
But the people are very good
at avoiding accountability.

00:43:18.650 --> 00:43:22.330
And if you don't have a
really clear plan of action

00:43:22.330 --> 00:43:26.580
to come up against it, it
probably won't work well.

00:43:26.580 --> 00:43:28.495
The Russia thing is
maybe the only thing

00:43:28.495 --> 00:43:30.870
that there will be a little
bit of accountability for it.

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:33.600
You have the Inspector
General report coming out

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:34.980
within the next month.

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:38.850
Inspector General reports
are extremely boring

00:43:38.850 --> 00:43:41.220
and they're written
almost in a way

00:43:41.220 --> 00:43:44.430
to preserve the agency
that they serve.

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:46.710
But these recent
Inspector General reports

00:43:46.710 --> 00:43:50.220
from the Department of Justice
have been absolutely explosive.

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:52.530
I mean, talking about
really bad things

00:43:52.530 --> 00:43:54.660
that James Comey has
done, recommending him

00:43:54.660 --> 00:43:57.150
for criminal prosecution,
even if that was declined

00:43:57.150 --> 00:43:58.620
by the Department of Justice.

00:43:58.620 --> 00:44:00.330
Saying about the
damage he's done

00:44:00.330 --> 00:44:05.620
to the Department of Justice
and whatnot and to the FBI.

00:44:05.620 --> 00:44:08.790
And I expect this next report
will be more of the same,

00:44:08.790 --> 00:44:10.840
and that there might be
actual actions taken.

00:44:10.840 --> 00:44:13.510
Even though it only is going
to deal with surveillance.

00:44:13.510 --> 00:44:17.500
And Attorney General Barr
and this prosecutor Durham

00:44:17.500 --> 00:44:20.493
really are looking into
the origins of the probe.

00:44:20.493 --> 00:44:21.910
And for me, I would
just almost be

00:44:21.910 --> 00:44:24.308
happy to just get
straight answers.

00:44:24.308 --> 00:44:26.350
It would also be nice to
hold people accountable.

00:44:26.350 --> 00:44:28.390
And if they don't hold
people accountable,

00:44:28.390 --> 00:44:31.510
I worry about, again, the
preservation of the republic.

00:44:31.510 --> 00:44:34.630
People have already seen,
in repeated fashion,

00:44:34.630 --> 00:44:36.280
that if you are with
one set of people

00:44:36.280 --> 00:44:38.712
you can get away with,
quite literally, murder.

00:44:38.712 --> 00:44:40.420
And if you're with
another set of people,

00:44:40.420 --> 00:44:43.420
any little thing you do
wrong will put you in prison.

00:44:43.420 --> 00:44:47.650
And that is not sustainable for
confidence in our institutions.

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:50.267
So I hope that even though
people don't want to hold

00:44:50.267 --> 00:44:52.600
people accountable, that they
recognize that it would be

00:44:52.600 --> 00:44:56.050
a much worse problem for
the country if they don't.

00:44:56.050 --> 00:44:58.120
SPEAKER 2: We have time
for one more question.

00:44:58.120 --> 00:45:01.370
AUDIENCE: OK, right.

00:45:01.370 --> 00:45:05.620
I recognize in your
talk there about

00:45:05.620 --> 00:45:10.260
the so-called Conservatives that
aren't quite so conservative.

00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:11.740
It's a cabal.

00:45:11.740 --> 00:45:13.680
They side with the Democrats.

00:45:13.680 --> 00:45:17.770
It's an expression of the
sort of run with the fox

00:45:17.770 --> 00:45:19.690
and hunt with the hounds.

00:45:19.690 --> 00:45:25.780
And you know, I get 20 pleas
from the Republican Party

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:30.220
to donate throughout the day,
I'm getting them right now.

00:45:30.220 --> 00:45:34.280
And then I had 41
representatives retiring

00:45:34.280 --> 00:45:36.940
and pulling out, now 20.

00:45:36.940 --> 00:45:41.470
So I think you're
absolutely right.

00:45:41.470 --> 00:45:49.110
You need people that are there
with conviction, not just one--

00:45:49.110 --> 00:45:49.610
.

00:45:49.610 --> 00:45:52.570
Question, You want a question.

00:45:52.570 --> 00:45:54.400
I'll give you a question.

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:56.860
Well, the issue of
a surrogate city,

00:45:56.860 --> 00:46:00.180
I thought we fought a
civil war about that.

00:46:00.180 --> 00:46:03.532
And when I talked to people
in the Department of Justice,

00:46:03.532 --> 00:46:04.990
and they assured
me they were going

00:46:04.990 --> 00:46:08.710
to go after individuals
who control the cities,

00:46:08.710 --> 00:46:12.650
because cities don't
declare their--

00:46:12.650 --> 00:46:17.390
it's people that make
these statements.

00:46:17.390 --> 00:46:22.720
And why hasn't the Justice
Department gone aggressively

00:46:22.720 --> 00:46:28.467
at those individual mayors that
are in charge of these cities?

00:46:28.467 --> 00:46:29.550
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: OK, yes.

00:46:29.550 --> 00:46:30.050
OK, thanks.

00:46:30.050 --> 00:46:31.800
I thought that was
about sanctuary cities.

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:35.450
Well, it is actually a
pretty complicated situation,

00:46:35.450 --> 00:46:37.780
depending on the laws at hand.

00:46:37.780 --> 00:46:41.350
And the Justice Department has
gone after some sanctuary city

00:46:41.350 --> 00:46:43.365
issues, and I think
they'll continue to do so.

00:46:43.365 --> 00:46:44.740
But if you don't
mind, I actually

00:46:44.740 --> 00:46:46.240
kind of want to
talk about something

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:49.200
else you just raised about
retiring representatives

00:46:49.200 --> 00:46:50.470
and whatnot.

00:46:50.470 --> 00:46:52.510
Which I think, this
is one of those things

00:46:52.510 --> 00:46:54.610
that people have a lot
of conventional wisdom

00:46:54.610 --> 00:46:56.440
in Washington DC,
that things must

00:46:56.440 --> 00:46:58.300
be really bad for
Republicans because you

00:46:58.300 --> 00:47:00.790
have all these Republican
representatives retiring.

00:47:00.790 --> 00:47:01.570
And that might be.

00:47:01.570 --> 00:47:03.528
It might be true that
those people who all have

00:47:03.528 --> 00:47:05.600
this viewpoint are correct.

00:47:05.600 --> 00:47:08.440
It is also true that
being elected to office

00:47:08.440 --> 00:47:11.200
does not give you a
lifetime appointment,

00:47:11.200 --> 00:47:13.060
and that sometimes
it's healthy for people

00:47:13.060 --> 00:47:15.890
to step down and be
replaced with other people.

00:47:15.890 --> 00:47:18.610
It has been a very
tumultuous period of time

00:47:18.610 --> 00:47:21.627
for people on the right and
also for people on the left.

00:47:21.627 --> 00:47:23.710
But since we're probably
more interested in what's

00:47:23.710 --> 00:47:26.230
happening on the
right, that disruption

00:47:26.230 --> 00:47:28.480
has caused a lot of
people to realize

00:47:28.480 --> 00:47:32.080
that old alliances weren't
as effective as they

00:47:32.080 --> 00:47:32.980
thought they were.

00:47:32.980 --> 00:47:35.188
And it has caused some people
to realize that they're

00:47:35.188 --> 00:47:36.880
out of touch with many voters.

00:47:36.880 --> 00:47:39.490
That's actually a great thing.

00:47:39.490 --> 00:47:42.790
And it is also related to
why we're here, in my view,

00:47:42.790 --> 00:47:44.560
that there are
certain people who

00:47:44.560 --> 00:47:46.840
have handled this moment
better than other people,

00:47:46.840 --> 00:47:49.420
certain institutions who have
handled the moment better.

00:47:49.420 --> 00:47:51.820
Part of it, I think,
actually is groups

00:47:51.820 --> 00:47:54.040
that are not embedded
in Washington

00:47:54.040 --> 00:47:57.940
DC seem to do a better job at
resisting the culture there.

00:47:57.940 --> 00:48:01.810
Hillsdale College other groups
there throughout the country.

00:48:01.810 --> 00:48:06.190
And we did just start a
graduate program out in DC.

00:48:06.190 --> 00:48:07.660
But the important
thing about that

00:48:07.660 --> 00:48:11.140
is, there is a unique
perspective that what ails

00:48:11.140 --> 00:48:13.270
a lot of what's
happening in our country

00:48:13.270 --> 00:48:16.030
is this reliance on the
administrative state

00:48:16.030 --> 00:48:18.970
over and against
our constitution.

00:48:18.970 --> 00:48:21.880
The way through is to
remember these constitutional

00:48:21.880 --> 00:48:23.470
principles, our
balance of government,

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:26.440
that the legislature should be
legislating, that they should

00:48:26.440 --> 00:48:28.780
have better oversight of
the executive agencies

00:48:28.780 --> 00:48:31.270
and enforcing our laws,
including border laws

00:48:31.270 --> 00:48:33.575
and sanctuary laws and whatnot.

00:48:33.575 --> 00:48:35.200
But a lot of people
haven't heard this.

00:48:35.200 --> 00:48:37.630
Despite all of the money
and effort that people

00:48:37.630 --> 00:48:41.330
have been sending to DC and all
that's been happening there,

00:48:41.330 --> 00:48:43.240
it's kind of a new
concept for people

00:48:43.240 --> 00:48:46.060
to realize how to take
on the bureaucracy

00:48:46.060 --> 00:48:48.520
and how important it
is for self-government.

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:51.580
And so I think this is
good that we are now

00:48:51.580 --> 00:48:53.753
focusing some
additional efforts in DC

00:48:53.753 --> 00:48:56.170
with this message that is a
little bit different than what

00:48:56.170 --> 00:48:59.680
has been happening
for a long time.

00:48:59.680 --> 00:49:01.410
Thank you.

